.SH "Implementation"
.PP
If nnu denotes the number of rows (variables), the non-zero entries of the i-th row (1 ≤ i ≤ nnu) are stored in \fBa(j)\fP where

.PP
\fBia(i)\fP ≤ j ≤ ia(i+1)-1\&.

.PP
In particular, according to the above-mentioned convention about the location of the diagonal element, \fBa(ia(i))\fP contains the diagonal entry of row i\&. Note that \fBia(1)\fP = 1 and ia(nnu+1) = nna+1 where nna denotes the total number of matrix entries stored\&.

.PP
ia, ja define the order of the off-diagonal elements in the solution matrix storage vector a (that holds them row by row)\&.

.PP
The pointer vector ja has to be defined so that \fBja(j)\fP (1 ≤ j ≤ nna) equals the original matrix’ column index of \fBa(j)\fP, ie, \fBa(j)\fP corresponds to the variable u(ja(j))\&. In particular, since \fBa(ia(i))\fP contains the diagonal entry of row i, we have \fBja(ia(i))\fP=i\&.

.PP
While, in the compressed row format, all diagonal entries must be stored, off-diagonal entries are only stored if they are non-zero\&. 
.br
